ation-type="journal">Choi, W.S., Y.M. Kim, C. Combs, M.A. Frohman, and M.A. Beaven. <year>2002</year>. Phospholipases D1 and D2 regulate different phases of exocytosis in mast cells. <source>J. Immunol.</source> <volume>168</volume>:<fpage>5682</fpage>&#x02013;5689.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12023367</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib11"><citation citation-type="journal">Chyb, S., P. Raghu, and R.C. Hardie. <year>1999</year>. Polyunsaturated fatty acids activate the <italic>Drosophila</italic> light-sensitive channels TRP and TRPL. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>397</volume>:<fpage>255</fpage>&#x02013;259.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9930700</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><citation citation-type="journal">Colley, N.J., J.A. Cassill, E.K. Baker, and C.S. Zuker. <year>1995</year>. Defective intracellular transport is the molecular basis of rhodopsin-dependent dominant retinal degeneration. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>92</volume>:<fpage>3070</fpage>&#x02013;3074.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7708777</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Deretic, D., V. Traverso, N. Parkins, F. Jackson, E.B. Rodriguez de Turco, and N. Ransom. <year>2004</year>. Phosphoinositides, ezrin/moesin, and rac1 regulate fusion of rhodopsin transport carriers in retinal photoreceptors. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>15</volume>:<fpage>359</fpage>&#x02013;370.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">13679519</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><citation citation-type="journal">Du, G., Y.M. Altshuller, N. Vitale, P. Huang, S. Chasserot-Golaz, A.J. Morris, M.F. Bader, and M.A. Frohman. <year>2003</year>. Regulation of phospholipase D1 subcellular cycling through coordination of multiple membrane association motifs. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>162</volume>:<fpage>305</fpage>&#x02013;315.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12876278</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><citation citation-type="journal">Frohman, M.A., and A.J. Morris. <year>1999</year>. Phospholipase D structure and regulation. <source>Chem. Phys. Lipids.</source> <volume>98</volume>:<fpage>127</fpage>&#x02013;140.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10358935</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal">Hardie, R.C. <year>2001</year>. Phototransduction in <italic>Drosophila melanogaster</italic>. <source>J. Exp. Biol.</source> <volume>204</volume>:<fpage>3403</fpage>&#x02013;3409.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11707492</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="journal">Hardie, R.C., and B. Minke. <year>1992</year>. The trp gene is essential for a light-activated Ca<sup>2+</sup> channel in <italic>Drosophila</italic> photoreceptors. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>8</volume>:<fpage>643</fpage>&#x02013;651.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1314617</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="journal">Hardie, R.C., and P. Raghu. <year>2001</year>. Visual transduction in <italic>Drosophila</italic>. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>413</volume>:<fpage>186</fpage>&#x02013;193.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11557987</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">Hardie, R.C., P. Raghu, S. Moore, M. Juusola, R.A. Baines, and S.T. Sweeney. <year>2001</year>. Calcium influx via TRP channels is required to maintain PIP2 levels in <italic>Drosophila</italic> photoreceptors. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>30</volume>:<fpage>149</fpage>&#x02013;159.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11343651</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Hardie, R.C., F. Martin, S. Chyb, and P. Raghu. <year>2003</year>. Rescue of light responses in the <italic>Drosophila</italic> &#x0201c;null&#x0201d; phospholipase C mutant, norpAP24, by the diacylglycerol kinase mutant, rdgA, and by metabolic inhibition. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>278</volume>:<fpage>18851</fpage>&#x02013;18858.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12621055</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">Harris, W.A., and W.S. Stark. <year>1977</year>. Hereditary retinal degeneration in <italic>Drosophila melanogaster</italic>. A mutant defect associated with the phototransduction process. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>69</volume>:<fpage>261</fpage>&#x02013;291.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">139462</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">Honda, A., M. Nogami, T. Yokozeki, M. Yamazaki, H. Nakamura, H. Watanabe, K. Kawamoto, K. Nakayama, A.J. Morris, M.A. Frohman, and Y. Kanaho. <year>1999</year>. Phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate 5-kinase alpha is a downstream effector of the small G protein ARF6 in membrane ruffle formation. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>99</volume>:<fpage>521</fpage>&#x02013;532.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10589680</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Hotta, Y., and S. Benzer. <year>1970</year>. Genetic dissection of the <italic>Drosophila</italic> nervous system by means of mosaics. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>67</volume>:<fpage>1156</fpage>&#x02013;1163.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">5274445</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Hsu, C.D., M.A. Whaley, K. Frazer, D.A. Miller, K.A. Mitchell, S.M. Adams, and J.E. O'Tousa. <year>2004</year>. Limited role of developmental programmed cell death pathways in <italic>Drosophila norpA</italic> retinal degeneration. <source>J. Neurosci.</source> <volume>24</volume>:<fpage>500</fpage>&#x02013;507.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14724249</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Inoue, H., T. Yoshioka, and Y. Hotta. <year>1985</year>. A genetic study of inositol trisphosphate involvement in phototransduction using <italic>Drosophila</italic> mutants. <source>Biochem. Biophys. Res. Commun.</source> <volume>132</volume>:<fpage>513</fpage>&#x02013;519.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2998372</pub-id><